%rec: default
# classes.rec: the classes a new hero can pick, one record each, in the order the class menu lists them.
# Fields:
#   internal_name     the class's id; its sprites are icons/actors.rec's class.<internal_name>_male / _female
#   name              what the class menu shows
#   gold              gold a new hero starts with
#   item              an item a new hero starts with, by items.rec internal_name; repeatable. Weapons and armour
#                     are equipped; "<item> pack" puts it in the pack instead of a hand
#   str / mag / dex / vit             a new hero's attributes; max_str / max_mag / max_dex / max_vit their caps
#   to_blk            chance to block with a shield, before dexterity and level
#   adj_life / adj_mana               the flat part of the starting life and mana
#   lvl_life / lvl_mana               life and mana gained per character level
#   chr_life / chr_mana               life per point of vitality, mana per point of magic, when it is raised
#   itm_life / itm_mana               the same, for vitality and magic that items give
#   potion_life / potion_mana         multiplier on what a healing / mana potion restores
#   spell_life        multiplier on the Healing spell; mana_cost multiplier on every spell's mana cost
#                     (the life/mana/potion/spell numbers are decimals: 1 = normal, 0.5 = half, as in devilutionX)
#   magic_to_hit / melee_to_hit / ranged_to_hit   the class's to-hit bonus for each kind of attack
#   spell_to_hit      extra to-hit for its magic missiles (Sorcerer 20, Bard 10)
#   attack_frames     attack animation length by weapon: "default=16 axe=20" (sword axe bow mace staff)
#   spell_frames / hit_frames         cast animation length, and hit recovery length
#   start_spells      the spells and skills the class can learn, by id, at the level a new hero knows them:
#                     "SPL_FIREBOLT=2 arrow_rain=0 ..." (0: learnable with skill points, not known yet). A skill is
#                     a D1 SPL_ or any id of the engine's skills (src/rules/skills.rec) or of the mod's spells.rec
#   skill_points      skill points a hero gains per level, spent on start_spells' skills (none: 0)
#   max_skill_level   how far skill points raise a skill; books and gear go past it
#   heal_boost        Healing and potions: 2 doubles the roll, 1 adds half, 0 neither
#   flags             what the class does differently, any of: critical_strike (level% of swings hit twice),
#                     trap_sense (sees traps), iron_skin (+level/4 AC), natural_resistance (+level to every
#                     resistance), dual_wield, trained_fists (bare hands hit level/2..level), parry (blocks with
#                     a staff, one one-hander or bare hands), full_missile_bonus (missiles get the whole damage
#                     bonus), half_shield (a shield without a weapon counts half), vitality_damage (+level*vit/100
#                     melee), shield_slows_bare_hands, flinch_resist (hit recovery needs level/4 more damage), and
#                     at most one melee damage formula: str_dex_damage, staff_and_fist_damage,
#                     blade_and_bow_damage, heavy_weapon_damage (none: level*str/100)
#   key               what spells.rec's effects call the class (default: name, lowercased)
#   skill             its class skill: repair, disarm, recharge, vision, identify or rage (none: no skill)
#   voice / female_voice              its PS_<voice>nn sound set (WARR, ROGUE, MAGE, MONK), male and female
#   death_sound       said on death instead of PS_<voice>71
#   text_prefix       its version of the quest books' texts: TEXT_<prefix>BONER
#   oily              what the Oily shrine gives it: "str 2"; repeatable
#   slain_hero        what the Slain Hero leaves it: an items.rec internal_name (a magic item of its type and
#                     look) or "book <SPL_>" (a spell book)
#
# The three classes this game is played with. Classes are content: the engine keeps no roster of them and reads
# each class' two sprites from this mod's actors.rec by internal_name (class.<internal_name>_male/_female).
#
# The hero model itself is the engine's one unified hero, so each class names the hero archetype it plays as
# (warrior / rogue / sorcerer) and carries its OWN display name over it. The Archer plays as the rogue and the
# Mage as the sorcerer, which is what they always were.

# THESE NUMBERS ARE A DESIGN CHOICE MADE DURING THE PORT. They are not a port of anything and they are meant to
# be tuned by playing the game.
#
# Heaven & Hell never had this table. Its branch used the `stats` package - strength/dexterity/intelligence plus
# health and mana resources - and it had neither growth curves nor
# caps nor an experience ladder. So nothing here could be carried over row for row, and copying Diablo's rows
# would have been a lie dressed as a port.
#
# What IS derived, and from what:
#   - starting str/mag/dex came from this file's old stats_strength / stats_intelligence /
#     stats_dexterity, doubled: H&H's values run ~5-15 where the engine's formulas expect ~10-30. Each class
#     keeps its shape - the Warrior stays str-heavy, the Archer dex-heavy, the Mage int-heavy.
#   - vit has no H&H equivalent, so it is derived from the old resource_health (20/15/10 -> 30/20/15).
#   - mana follows the old resource_mana (1/1/4): the Warrior is mana-poor, the Mage mana-rich.
#
# What is CHOSEN, by the role each class' archetype plays: the caps, the 1/64 life and mana curves, the to-hit
# numbers and the animation frame counts. Warrior tanky and mana-starved, Archer balanced, Mage mana-rich and
# fragile. The to-hit and frame columns have no H&H source at all and are set to what the engine's formulas are
# calibrated against for that archetype.

internal_name: warrior
name: Warrior
gold: 180
item: main_gauche
